 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Normally closed action",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "With no LED current the output MOSFETs conduct and pins 3 and 4 are joined through RON. LED current of 0.5 mA or more opens the output; once open, it stays open until the LED current falls to its release point; that point is 0.05 mA or more. Turn-on and turn-off each take up to 10 ms at 2 mA drive. The output turns off as the LED current rises and turns back on after the LED current is removed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching time definitions",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Turn-off time toff runs from the rising edge of the LED current to the load current falling to 10 %; turn-on time ton runs from the falling edge of the LED current to the load current reaching 90 %."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The load switch is a pair of MOSFETs biased by a photovoltaic die; it carries AC or DC in either direction, is arc-free with no snubber, has no moving parts, generates no EMI/RFI and is immune to radiated EM fields. The load current against load voltage is a straight line through zero in both directions. The continuous load current holds at 75 mA to about 55 °C and falls to 50 mA at 85 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input drive at temperature",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The LED current needed to switch rises with temperature, from about 0.11 mA at -40 °C to about 0.28 mA at 85 °C. For operation above 60 °C the recommended LED drive is 2 mA. The input is TTL/CMOS compatible and takes up to 5 V of reverse voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Standards and uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Built for security systems compliant with EN50130-4: passive infrared detectors, data signalling and sensor circuits. Also named: instrument multiplexers, data acquisition, I/O subsystems, utility meters, patient/equipment isolation in medical equipment, aerospace and industrial controls."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Assembly",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Pick and place and wave soldering are both supported. No-clean flux is preferred; washing off flux residue is acceptable, and a short drying bake may be needed after a post-reflow wash, as silicone serves as the optical waveguide. Chlorinated or fluorinated solvents and fluxes, and ultrasonic cleaning, are not to be used. Lead-free (e3), RoHS. Tape and reel tolerances follow EIA-481-2 except where a tolerance is given."
  }
 ],
